    "evidence_text": "We discuss possible applications of this dataset and present the results obtained for the first lexical baseline system for Portuguese NLI.\n\nIntroduction\tSeveral learner corpora have been compiled for English, such as the International Corpus of Learner English  .\n\nThe importance of such resources has been increasingly recognized across a variety of research areas, from Second Language Acquisition to Natural Language Processing.\n\nRecently, we have seen substantial growth in this area and new corpora for languages other than English have\n\nfor research, particularly for Native Language Identification (NLI), a task that has received much attention in recent years.\n\nNLI works by identifying language use patterns that are common to groups of speakers of the same native language.\n\nA major motivation for NLI is studying second language acquisition.\n\nHowever, there are limitations to using existing Portuguese data for NLI.\n\nThe different data formats used by each corpus presents yet another challenge to their\n\nusage.\n\nIn this paper we present NLI-PT, a dataset collected for Portuguese NLI.\n\nThe dataset is made freely available for research purposes.\n\nRelated Work\tNLI has attracted a lot of attention in recent years.\n\nDue to the availability of suitable data, as discussed earlier, this attention has been particularly focused on English.\n\nRecent NLI studies on languages other than English include Arabic  and Chinese  ,  .\n\nTo\n\nThe three corpora contain written productions from learners of Portuguese with different proficiency levels and native languages (L1s).\n\nThe main variable we used for text selection was the presence of specific L1s.\n\nSince the three corpora consider different L1s, we decided to use\n\nthe L1s present in the largest corpus, COPLE2, as the reference.\n\nThe number of texts from each L1 is presented in Table 2 .\n\nThose topics are presented to the students in order to produce a written text.\n\nFor this reason, the topics\n\nDue to the different distribution of topics in the source corpora, the 148 topics in the dataset are not represented uniformly.\n\nThis variability affects also text length.\n\nThe longest text has 787 tokens and the shortest has only 16 tokens.\n\n21-30 tokens and so on) (Figure 2 ).\n\nPreprocessing and annotation of texts\tAs demonstrated earlier, these learner corpora use different formats.\n\nPEAPL2 and Leiria corpus are compiled in TXT format.\n\nIn both corpora, the TXT files contain the student version with special annotations from the transcription.\n\nAs a second\n\nstep, we proceeded to the linguistic annotation of the texts using different NLP tools.\n\nWe annotated the dataset at two levels: Part of Speech (POS) and syntax.\n\nWe performed the annotation with freely available tools for the Portuguese language.\n\nConcerning syntactic annotations, we included constituency and dependency annotations.\n\nFor constituency parsing, we used the LX Parser, and for dependency, the DepPattern toolkit  .\n\nHere are a few examples of applications in which the dataset can be used:\n\nIn this experiment we included the five L1s\n\nWe evaluated the model using stratified 10-fold cross-validation, achieving 70% accuracy.\n\nbe used for different research purposes beyond NLI such as grammatical error correction and CALL.\n\nIn future work we would like to include more texts in the dataset following the same methodology and annotation.",
